WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:05.080
There's a billionaire named Leon Cooperman
who cried on live television and there's a

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:06.080
lot of sort of

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:10.480
misreporting about why he was crying or what
the entire point of the thing was.

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:12.970
If you've not heard of Kuperman, he's a hedge
fund guy.

00:00:12.970 --> 00:00:14.960
He runs Omega advisors.

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:19.460
He's been sort of going back and forth with
Elizabeth Warren saying, I don't need Elizabeth

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:23.699
Warren telling me I'm a deadbeat and that
billionaires are deadbeats.

00:00:23.699 --> 00:00:27.609
Arguing that the vilification of billionaires
makes no sense to him and that the world is

00:00:27.609 --> 00:00:32.029
a better place because of people like bill
Gates and Michael Bloomberg and other rich

00:00:32.029 --> 00:00:33.029
people.

00:00:33.029 --> 00:00:34.250
This happened on CNBC.

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:35.550
Let's look at it and then discuss.

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:42.850
I think, um, I mean I think it's obvious people
can not only see the emotion on your face,

00:00:42.850 --> 00:00:46.600
but hear it in your voice when you talk about
this Lee, why

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:58.320
I care.

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:05.070
That's it.

00:01:05.070 --> 00:01:11.530
But in all that you have said of of where
the economy is, where the stock market is,

00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:16.670
uh, the wealth that has been created since
the president was inaugurated through, through

00:01:16.670 --> 00:01:23.270
the stock market appreciation, you still think
it would be a better idea if he did not run

00:01:23.270 --> 00:01:27.112
for reelection at this particular time where
I think [inaudible], like

00:01:27.112 --> 00:01:29.330
I said, you know, you're looking to get me
into trouble.

00:01:29.330 --> 00:01:31.020
So listen, there's no shame in crying.

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:35.080
I mean, this is just, this is 0% about, Oh
look at the guy crying.

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:36.560
It's not manly to cry.

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:38.340
It's not about anything like that at all.

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:42.130
And there are also headlines that have promoted
this as Leon Cooperman is crying over being

00:01:42.130 --> 00:01:43.670
taxed or whatever.

00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:45.009
It's not really about that either.

00:01:45.009 --> 00:01:49.220
He signed a pledge to give away like all of
his money or almost all of his money.

00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:56.000
This is about, first of all, a guy who's 76
and has more than $3 billion crying because

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:02.400
he sees Elizabeth Warren's policy ideas of
a wealth tax as bad for the country.

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:08.090
And more importantly, he's crying because
he seems to feel that these policies are personal

00:02:08.090 --> 00:02:15.400
attacks on billionaires as individual people,
as being individually bad people.

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:19.690
This is what I think the main point that should
be made about all of this is and the idea

00:02:19.690 --> 00:02:22.750
of a wealth tax and inequality in dealing
with it.

00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:25.790
This is not about billionaires personally
being bad people.

00:02:25.790 --> 00:02:30.060
This is about fixing a broken system that
allows this level of inequality that allows

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:34.420
a country as rich as the United States to
still have people starving or unable to meet

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:40.849
basic expenses or unable afford an unexpected
$400 expense or at risk of bankruptcy if they

00:02:40.849 --> 00:02:44.519
get sick or have a medical condition or some
kind of car accident.

00:02:44.519 --> 00:02:45.950
That's what this is about.

00:02:45.950 --> 00:02:52.400
So I understand Leon Cooperman taking it personally
to a degree, but someone needs to tell him

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:53.690
that simply not what it's about.

00:02:53.690 --> 00:02:55.769
I'll even go further.

00:02:55.769 --> 00:02:59.769
I am open to the idea that Leon Cooperman
is a pretty good guy.

00:02:59.769 --> 00:03:00.769
At a personal level.

00:03:00.769 --> 00:03:06.140
I might not agree with his politics, but at
the same time, he is part of the system that

00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:13.750
allows the raping and pillaging of the, the,
the, the greater good and the middle-class

00:03:13.750 --> 00:03:17.950
for the gain of the financial industry and
really rich dudes like him.

00:03:17.950 --> 00:03:23.340
Now, how could he both be an okay guy and
part of such a corrupt system and process?

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:24.860
This is the way it is.

00:03:24.860 --> 00:03:28.860
Personally, he's pledged to give away some
huge portion of his fortune or all of it.

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:29.860
Okay.

00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:30.860
That's a good thing.

00:03:30.860 --> 00:03:31.860
He's a philanthropists.

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:32.860
That's fine.

00:03:32.860 --> 00:03:36.500
But at the same time, he was one of the top
executives at Goldman Sachs during the time

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:42.189
that they were engaging in all sorts of criminal
activities for self enrichment, including

00:03:42.189 --> 00:03:48.349
um, uh, insider trading, uh, manipulating
energy prices, mortgage fraud, accounting

00:03:48.349 --> 00:03:49.349
fraud.

00:03:49.349 --> 00:03:53.680
So he's taking it personally, maybe because
he doesn't understand, maybe he's taking it

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:58.720
personally because he has a guilty conscience,
but the policy prescriptions of taxing wealth

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:02.920
or capital gains or whatever, these are not
attacks on people personally, or at least

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:04.510
they shouldn't be seen that way.

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:08.250
It's about even good people can be cogs.

00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:13.840
In a system that is fundamentally immoral
and the immorality of the system needs to

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:14.840
change.

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:19.220
It's not necessarily the people, although
many of the people could use some realignments

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:21.460
to their moral campuses as well.

00:04:21.460 --> 00:04:23.760
And so I think that this is an interesting
video.

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:27.960
I think that a lot of the coverage of it has
maybe misunderstood what this is and isn't

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:33.950
about, but it actually gives us some insight
into some of the resistance that exists out

00:04:33.950 --> 00:04:38.060
there to the policies of people like Elizabeth
Warren and Bernie Sanders, which is that it

00:04:38.060 --> 00:04:44.060
is being taken personally and it really shouldn't
be, uh, because this is about fixing a system,

00:04:44.060 --> 00:04:47.810
not about calling individual people necessarily
bad people.

00:04:47.810 --> 00:04:51.080
That being said, industry is filled with horrible
people.

00:04:51.080 --> 00:04:56.620
Uh, if you look at, you know, executives at
fossil fuel companies who knew the damage

00:04:56.620 --> 00:05:00.971
that their business was doing to the planet
and to people's health for decades, and they

00:05:00.971 --> 00:05:06.100
covered it up and continued raping and pillaging
the resources of the planet at the expense

00:05:06.100 --> 00:05:10.520
of people's health and the planet's health,
uh, that's not something a good person does.

00:05:10.520 --> 00:05:14.680
Uh, but we should be able to think about these
things discreetly and separate them out and

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:17.580
really make more sense than what some of the
salacious headlines seem to be implying.

